    "result_text": "The build is green \u2014 both tests pass (`Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change makes locality derivation prefer the postcode over the city.\n\n**What I changed** \u2014 `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java`, the `toLocality(Owner)` method:\n- Added a `REGION_POSTCODES` table (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099).\n- `toLocality` now parses the owner's postcode and returns the region whose inclusive range contains it. It falls back to the existing city-to-region table (and finally `UNKNOWN`) only when the postcode is absent, non-numeric, or in no known range.\n\nKnown cities still resolve the same (Sydney @ 2000 \u2192 NSW), while a shared/unknown city with a VIC-range postcode now correctly disambiguates to VIC.\n\n**One subtlety worth flagging:** my first attempt used a separate `regionFromPostcode(String):String` helper as a `default` interface method. MapStruct scans interface default methods for candidate mapping methods and treated it as a generic String\u2192String mapper, silently injecting it into *every* string field of the generated `toOwner` impl \u2014 nulling out firstName, city, telephone, etc., and causing owner creation to 500 with a `ConstraintViolationException`. I resolved this by inlining the postcode logic directly into `toLocality` (whose `Owner`\u2192`String` signature MapStruct won't misapply), which fixed the create path and made all tests pass.",
